2.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. A mutation in region 1 preventing its binding to region 2}

The trp leader transcript contains four segments that can base-pair to form alternative RNA hairpins (1-2 pause, 2-3 antiterminator, or 3-4 terminator). Under tryptophan-replete conditions the ribosome translates the leader peptide to its stop codon, thereby covering segment 2; segment 3 is therefore free to pair with segment 4 and form the intrinsic terminator.  

A mutation that abolishes pairing between segments 1 and 2 leaves segment 2 unpaired even when the ribosome is not stalled. Segment 2 is then able to pair with the newly synthesized segment 3, locking the antiterminator structure in place and thereby sequestering segment 3 so that the 3-4 terminator cannot form. Transcription therefore continues through the operon regardless of tryptophan level.  

All other choices either favor terminator formation (B), alter sequences outside the stem itself (C), increase termination (D), or reduce transcription initiation altogether (E).}

