6.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The trp leader transcript can adopt mutually exclusive secondary structures, with 3:4 forming the intrinsic terminator hairpin (followed by the run of U residues) that causes attenuation. Under tryptophan-replete conditions the ribosome does not stall at the Trp codons inside segment 1, proceeds into segment 2, and thereby prevents formation of the 2:3 antiterminator; segment 3 is therefore free to pair with segment 4.  

A mutation that destroys base-pairing potential between segments 1 and 2 leaves segment 2 free to pair with segment 3 as soon as segment 3 is transcribed. The resulting 2:3 antiterminator sequesters segment 3, so the 3:4 terminator cannot form even when tryptophan is high. Consequently RNA polymerase continues through the operon.  

The other options either favor terminator formation (B), alter termination efficiency without preventing hairpin formation (C), increase rather than decrease attenuation (D), or reduce transcription initiation altogether (E).}

8.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The trp leader transcript contains four regions that can pair to form alternative RNA structures. The 3-4 hairpin is the functional terminator; its formation causes RNA polymerase to stop. Under tryptophan-replete conditions the ribosome translates the entire leader peptide, covering region 2 when regions 3 and 4 are transcribed; this leaves 3 free to pair with 4 and terminate transcription.

A mutation that prevents region 1 from base-pairing with region 2 leaves region 2 unpaired. Consequently, region 2 is available to pair with region 3 even when the ribosome has reached the stop codon. Formation of the 2-3 antiterminator sequesters region 3, so the 3-4 terminator cannot form and transcription continues. None of the other mutations block 3-4 pairing under high-tryptophan conditions: B favors the terminator, C alters the downstream run of U residues rather than the stem-loop itself, D does not change ribosome coverage of region 2, and E merely reduces initiation.

Thus only mutation A reliably prevents terminator formation when tryptophan is high.}

