14.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. A mutation in region 1 preventing its binding to region 2

The canonical four-segment model still holds under high-Trp conditions: the ribosome reaches the trpL stop codon and covers segment 2, freeing segment 3 to pair with segment 4 and form the terminator hairpin. A mutation that blocks 1–2 pairing leaves segment 2 constitutively available to base-pair with the newly transcribed segment 3, locking in the 2–3 antiterminator regardless of ribosome position. This directly sequesters segment 3 and prevents 3–4 stem-loop formation, allowing read-through.  

Option C only alters the downstream U-run that follows an already-formed 3–4 hairpin; it can weaken polymerase release but does not change which hairpin is chosen by the attenuation mechanism itself. B, D and E likewise either favor the terminator or leave hairpin selection untouched. The group’s shared reasoning therefore continues to single out A as the mutation that reliably blocks terminator formation.}

